To guarantee that the 10GB of RAM allocated on the Linux client remains completely shielded from local attacks (including high-privileged memory dumpers with root access and advanced REA agents), 
the kernel module leverages five native, low-level mechanisms:

1. Page Table Isolation & Page Attribute Manipulation

When the module allocates the memory block using alloc_pages(), it directly modifies the attribute control bits within the CPU page tables via the kernel's memory management (mm) functions.
• Mechanism: The driver switches the target page status to _PAGE_NX (No-Execute) and strips away standard user-space read/write permissions.
• Anti-REA Effect: The Linux user-space perceives this memory range as completely non-existent or inaccessible. Any local read attempt triggers an immediate hardware exception (SIGSEGV / Page Fault), 
while only the kernel-space network transceiver (DMA) retains the ability to read and write directly to the physical addresses.

2. Kernel Memory Protection Keys (MPK / PKEYs)

By leveraging hardware primitives embedded in modern CPUs (such as Intel PKEYs), the Linux Kernel can isolate active memory pages down to the execution thread level.
• Mechanism: The module binds the Memory Custody allocation pages to a dedicated protection key (pkey_alloc).
• Anti-REA Effect: Even if a malicious actor successfully executes an exploit to inject code inside the kernel-space, 
the physical CPU blocks all access to the memory blocks unless the invading execution thread possesses the explicit hardware key activated within the CPU's PKRU register.

3. Absolute Isolation via Memory Subsumption (MemBlock + movable_node)

Instead of competing for DRAM allocations with the standard Linux memory manager (the Buddy Allocator), the framework completely removes the designated hardware allocation from the global ecosystem.
• Mechanism: During early initialization or boot sequences (leveraging the memblock infrastructure), the driver claims the physical RAM address space and forcefully excises it from the logical ZONE_NORMAL and ZONE_DMA32 pools.
• Anti-REA Effect: To the host operating system, those 10GB of RAM physically cease to exist. 
Standard memory-scraping tools (such as the dd if=/dev/mem interface) are structurally unable to index or scan the hijacked address space because it has been fully omitted from the Linux memory translation maps.

4. Kernel-Space eBPF & Netfilter Hooking (Network Bus Hardening)

To neutralize localized Man-in-the-Middle network exploits, such as packet capture attempts via tcpdump or Wireshark on the client's virtual network interface card.
• Mechanism: The kernel driver attaches an eBPF (Extended Berkeley Packet Filter) routine directly into the physical network interface controller layer using the XDP (eXpress Data Path) framework.
• Anti-REA Effect: Inbound encrypted Memory Custody packets arriving from the cloud are intercepted and processed the exact microsecond they hit the physical interface. 
The eBPF routine reroutes the payload directly into the isolated memory vault and drops the source network packets before they enter the standard Linux networking stack, 
completely breaking the capability of local sniffers to monitor the internet data pipeline.

5. Active Cache Line Purging (Sanitization via CLFLUSH Primitives)

To prevent remnants of AI processing arrays or proprietary software state maps from persisting within the physical CPU memory hierarchies (L1/L2/L3 caches), where local hardware-scraping components could attempt access.
• Mechanism: Immediately following the execution cycle of each vertical buffer chunk, the kernel driver issues the machine-level assembly instruction CLFLUSH (Cache Line Flush) coupled with an MFENCE (Memory Fence) serialization barrier.
• Anti-REA Effect: This forces the hardware processor to physically purge data remnants out of its volatile silicon registers, guaranteeing total ephemeral execution bounds and zero operational footprint at runtime.


